On 2013-12-05 Thu 05:59, Dennis Murphy wrote:
> Is this what you were after?
>
> ggplot(data=stuff, mapping=aes(x=eks, y=Why)) +
>    geom_tile(mapping = aes(fill = GRUEP)) +
>    geom_point(mapping = aes(color = EVENNESS), shape=16, size=5) +
>    scale_fill_manual(values = c("pink", "yellow")) +
>    scale_color_manual(values = c("green", "red"))
>
> If so, notice where the fill and color aesthetics were assigned. In
> your plot code. You had color and fill mapped to *all* subsequent
> layers - i.e., color and fill were implicitly mapped to both
> geom_tile() and geom_point(). The above code assigns fill to
> geom_tile() and color to geom_point().
>
> Dennis
>
> On Wed, Dec 4, 2013 at 8:32 PM, Jacob Wegelin <jacobw...@fastmail.fm> wrote:
>> Alternate subject: Eliminate meaningless plot character from legend
>>
>> In the example below, I want two legends which clearly give information
>> about 2 different factors.  One legend should be for the "fill" and the
>> other for the "color".
>>
>> One solution, as I show, is to explicitly use layer().
>>
>> But I think Hadley wrote that he might eventually deprecate the layer()
>> function. (Also, explicit use of layer() is not well documented.) Is there
>> another way to achieve the same result?
>>
>> stuff<-data.frame(eks=1:10)
>> stuff$Why<-stuff$eks
>> stuff$GRUEP<-factor(with(stuff, ifelse(eks<5, "lo", "HI")))
>> stuff$EVENNESS<- factor( ifelse(((stuff$eks) %% 2)<1, "even", "odd"))
>> print(summary(stuff)) print(stuff)
>> library(ggplot2)
>>
>> # Bad: Meaningless plot character in the "GRUEP" legend and meaningless
>> black in the "EVENNESS" legend:
>>
>> print(
>>         ggplot(data=stuff, mapping=aes(x=eks, y=Why, fill=GRUEP,
>> color=EVENNESS)) + geom_tile() + geom_point(shape=16, size=5)
>>                 + scale_fill_manual(
>>                         values=c(
>>                                 "lo"="yellow"
>>                                 ,
>>                                 "HI"="pink"
>>                                 )
>>                         )
>>                 + scale_color_manual(
>>                         values=c(
>>                                 "even"="green"
>>                                 ,
>>                                 "odd"="red"
>>                                 )
>>                         )
>> )
>>
>> # Solution using layer():
>>
>> layerGRUEP<-layer(
>>         data=stuff
>>         , mapping=aes(x=eks, y=Why, fill=GRUEP)
>>         , geom="tile"
>>         )
>> layerEVENNESS<-layer(
>>         data=stuff
>>         , mapping=aes(x=eks, y=Why, color=EVENNESS)
>>         , geom="point"
>>         )
>> print(
>>         ggplot()
>>         + layerGRUEP
>>         + layerEVENNESS
>>         + scale_fill_manual(
>>                 values=c(
>>                         "lo"="yellow"
>>                         , "HI"="pink"
>>                         )
>>                 )
>>         + scale_color_manual(
>>                 values=c(
>>                         "odd"="green"
>>                         ,"even"="red"
>>                         )
>>                 )
>> )
>>
